      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Release of circulating malondialdehyde (MDA)-modified low-density lipoprotein (LDL) may reflect endothelial injury or plaque instability."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the usefulness of MDA-modified LDL for identifying patients with unstable angina and acute myocardial infarction (AMI)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Blinded comparison of MDA-modified LDL, C-reactive protein, and troponin I followed by multiple receiver operating curve analysis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "University hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "A total of 104 consecutive patients with acute coronary syndromes (42 with unstable angina and 62 with AMI), and 64 patients with stable coronary artery disease (CAD) without evidence of ischemia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Ability of MDA-modified LDL, C-reactive protein, and troponin I to discriminate patients with stable CAD, unstable angina, or AMI."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Malondialdehyde-modified LDL (chi2 = 10.2; P = .001), but not troponin I or C-reactive protein, discriminated between stable CAD and unstable angina. Troponin I (chi2 = 14.5; P<.001), but not MDA-modified LDL or C-reactive protein, discriminated between unstable angina and AMI. Both MDA-modified LDL and troponin I (chi2 = 14.5; P<.001 and chi2 = 5.3; P = .02, respectively) but not C-reactive protein discriminated between stable CAD and AMI. The sensitivity of MDA-modified LDL was 95% for unstable angina and 95% for AMI, with a specificity of 95%. Values for troponin I were 38% and 90%, respectively, with a specificity of 95%. The combination of MDA-modified LDL and troponin I had a sensitivity of 98% for unstable angina and 100% for AMI, with a specificity of 99%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The combination of MDA-modified LDL, which may reflect endothelial injury or plaque instability, and troponin I, which reflects myocardial cell injury, allows better discrimination between stable CAD and acute coronary syndromes than troponin I alone."
        }
      ]},
